.TH LLMFS 4
.SH NAME
llmfs \- interface to LLM file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B llmfs
[
.B -D
] [
.BI -m " mntpt"
] [
.BI -k " apikey"
] [
.BI -M " model"
]
.PP
.B /n/llm/clone
.br
.B /n/llm/info
.br
.BI /n/llm/ n /ctl
.br
.BI /n/llm/ n /data
.br
.BI /n/llm/ n /status
.br
.BI /n/llm/ n /system
.br
.BI /n/llm/ n /tools
.br
.BI /n/llm/ n /tool_choice
.br
.BI /n/llm/ n /messages/clone
.br
.BI /n/llm/ n /messages/ m /role
.br
.BI /n/llm/ n /messages/ m /content
.br
.BI /n/llm/ n /messages/ m /name
.br
.BI /n/llm/ n /messages/ m /tool_call_id
.br
.BI /n/llm/ n /messages/ m /tool_calls
.br
.BI /n/llm/ n /messages/ m /finish_reason
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Llmfs
provides a way to interact with an LLM over a filesystem interface.
It serves its hierarchy on
.I mntpt
(default
.BR /n/llm ),
issuing requests to the OpenRouter chat completions API
.RB ( https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions ).
The
.B -D
option enables Styx server tracing.
.B -M
sets the default model used for new connections
(default
.BR openai/gpt-4.1-nano ).
.B -k
supplies the OpenRouter API key;
if absent, the key is read from
.BR /env/OPENROUTER_API_KEY .
.PP
The top of the hierarchy contains a
.B clone
file, an
.B info
file, and zero or more numbered directories.
Each numbered directory represents a distinct connection
to the LLM. Opening the
.B clone
file reserves a connection: it is equivalent to opening the
.B ctl
file of an unused connection directory, creating a new one
if necessary.
.PP
A connection directory contains
.BR ctl ,
.BR data ,
.BR status ,
.BR system ,
.BR tools ,
.BR tool_choice ,
and a
.B messages
subdirectory described below.
.PP
The file
.B ctl
controls a connection.
When read, it returns the decimal number
.I n
of its connection directory.
Thus, opening and reading
.B clone
allocates a connection directory and reveals the number of the
allocated directory, allowing the other files to be named (eg,
.BI /n/llm/ n /messages\fR).
.PP
.B Ctl
accepts the following textual commands:
.TP
.BI "temp " n
Sampling temperature
.RI ( n
real, typically 0\(en2). Issue before
.BR send .
.TP
.BI "top " n
Nucleus sampling probability
.RI ( top_p ),
real between 0 and 1.
.TP
.BI "max_tokens " n
Limit the number of tokens to generate.
.TP
.BI "seed " n
Sampling seed.
.TP
.BI "model " name
Override the model for this connection.
.TP
.BI "tool_choice " v
Set the request's
.B tool_choice
field. Value is
.BR auto ,
.BR none ,
.BR required ,
or a function name.
.TP
.BI "transform " on|off
Toggle OpenRouter's
.B middle-out
context-length transform (default on).
With
.B middle-out
enabled, OpenRouter drops middle messages when the request
exceeds the model context.
.TP
.B send
Snapshot the current
.BR system ,
.BR tools ,
.BR tool_choice ,
and
.B messages
contents and start an API call. Sets
.B status
to
.B Generating
until the response arrives. Subsequent writes to
.B system
or
.B tools
do not affect the in-flight call.
.TP
.B cancel
Abort an in-flight call. State returns to
.B Idle
and any pending reads on the unborn assistant turn return an
error.
.TP
.BI "trim " n
Drop the
.I n
oldest non-empty messages from
.BR messages/ .
.TP
.BI "trim keep " k
Drop oldest messages until at most
.I k
remain.
Neither form of
.B trim
touches
.B system
or
.BR tools .
.TP
.B reset
Return the connection to the idle state, clearing
.BR system ,
.BR tools ,
.BR tool_choice ,
.BR data ,
and
.BR messages/ .
.PP
The
.B data
file is the simple one-shot interface (no tools, no multi-turn).
It must be opened separately for reading and for writing.
Writing to
.B data
appends to a plain prompt; closing the writer starts generation.
Reading
.B data
returns the generated text once generation completes;
reads issued while generation is in progress block until it
finishes. The
.B data
interface does not interact with
.BR system ,
.BR tools ,
or
.BR messages/ .
.PP
The
.B messages
directory is the multi-turn interface. It contains a
.B clone
file (open and read to allocate a new turn slot, returning the
turn index
.IR m )
and one numbered subdirectory per allocated turn.
Each turn subdirectory contains the per-message scalar files:
.TP
.B role
One of
.BR user ", " developer ", " assistant ", " tool .
For turns appended by the server in response to a
.B send
the role is always
.BR assistant .
.TP
.B content
Plain-text message content. Read-write for caller-created turns,
read-only for assistant turns. A read on the
.B content
file of the
.B not-yet-existing
assistant turn (turn index equal to the next free slot at
.B send
time) blocks until generation completes.
.TP
.B name
Optional name attached to the message.
.TP
.B tool_call_id
For
.B role=tool
turns: the
.B id
from the assistant's
.B tool_calls
entry that this turn answers.
Writing to
.B tool_call_id
on a turn whose role is unset implies
.BR role=tool .
.TP
.B tool_calls
Read-only. For assistant turns: the JSON array of
.B tool_calls
returned by the API, verbatim. Empty if the assistant produced
plain text.
.TP
.B finish_reason
Read-only. For assistant turns: one of
.BR stop ", " tool_calls ", " length ", " content_filter ", " error .
.PP
A turn directory may be removed
.RB ( "remove " m )
to drop just that turn from the conversation; remaining turns
keep their indices (gaps are allowed).
.PP
The files
.B system
and
.B tools
hold the system prompt (plain text) and the tool catalogue
(markdown, see
.B Tools format
below). Both are read/write at any time during the connection;
each
.B "ctl send"
takes a private snapshot of their current contents, so a write
that lands while a call is in flight is picked up by the next
.B send
without disturbing the running call. Writes that begin at
offset 0 replace the previous contents; later writes append.
.PP
.B tool_choice
holds the value passed to the API as
.BR tool_choice .
.PP
The read-only
.B status
file gives the current connection state:
.BR Idle ", " Prompting ", " Generating ", or " Done .
The read-only
.B info
file gives basic metadata about the default model
(includes
.B context_length
which is the size of the model's input window).
.SH TOOLS FORMAT
The
.B tools
file is markdown. One H1 header per tool; the body lines under
the header are the tool description; a fenced
.B json
code block holds the JSON Schema for the tool's parameters.
A tool with no fenced block gets the schema
.BR {"type":"object","properties":{}} .
For example:
.PP
.EX
# get_weather
Get the current weather for a location.

\\`\\`\\`json
{"type":"object",
 "properties":{"location":{"type":"string","description":"City"}},
 "required":["location"]}
\\`\\`\\`

# get_time
Return the current time in ISO 8601.
.EE
.SH TOOL CALL LOOP
After
.B send
the server appends an
.B assistant
turn to
.BR messages/ .
If
.B finish_reason
is
.B tool_calls
the assistant turn's
.B tool_calls
file holds a JSON array such as
.PP
.EX
[{"id":"call_abc","type":"function",
  "function":{"name":"get_weather","arguments":"{\\"location\\":\\"Paris\\"}"}}]
.EE
.PP
For each entry the caller executes the function locally, allocates
a new turn under
.BR messages/clone ,
sets
.B tool_call_id
to the entry's
.BR id ,
writes the function result to
.BR content ,
and writes
.B send
to
.B ctl
again. The loop terminates when
.B finish_reason
is
.BR stop .
.SH SOURCE
.B llmfs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR styxservers (2),
.IR mhttp (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A
.B write
to
.B ctl
returns an error if a command is malformed or unknown, or if
.B send
is issued with no
.B system
prompt and no messages.
A
.B read
from a per-message scalar file returns an error if the upstream
API call fails; the error text describes the failure (HTTP
status, dial error, parse error, or
.B "tools: ..."
for a malformed tools markdown block).
A read from
.B data
or from a not-yet-created
.B assistant
turn blocks until generation completes or the call is
.BR cancel led.
.SH BUGS
Token counts are not tracked locally, so context-length overflow
is detected only by the API's 4xx response. Use
.B "ctl trim"
or rely on
.B middle-out
to keep requests within the window.
